  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Daily chances for showers and thunderstorms \ninto the middle of the week. There is a risk for strong to \nsevere thunderstorms, as well as a marginal risk for localized \nheavy rainfall leading to flash flooding.\n\nThe risk for severe thunderstorms has ended across the area, and\ntherefore the remaining portion of the Severe Thunderstorm Watch\nhas been cancelled. Convection will continue to exit portions of\nDelmarva into far southeastern New Jersey early this evening.\n\nThe trend for lower instability has continued for Tuesday given\nthe faster evolution of the synoptic features (cold front and \nshortwave trough). Tuesday's activity will be displaced to our \nsouthwest. With that said, we could still see some convection \npotential, or even get the remnants of whatever occurs upstream \nlater in the evening. So while the threat of severe storms now \nappears to be lower for Tuesday, we could still get showers and \nstorms in some form. Details for Tuesday are even more uncertain.\nSPC expanded the Marginal Risk (level 1 out of 5) farther \nnortheast into the Philly metro area, and the entire area \nremains in the Marginal Risk (level 1 out of 4) for excessive \nrainfall.\n\nA cold front looks to stall to our northwest Tuesday into Wednesday.\nMultiple waves look to ride along the stalled boundary which will\nsupport diurnally driven convection continuing at least through\nWednesday, and possible Thursday. The front looks to push through\nby late Thursday, and a much drier and cooler airmass will arrive\nby Friday and Saturday as a result. This will bring us a more \npronounced break in convective potential and more pleasant weather\nconditions.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid conditions will persist into the \nmiddle of the week, but humidity will not be as oppressive as \nrecent days.\n\nA relatively stagnant synoptic pattern will remain in place \nacross our region. Quasi zonal mid and upper level flow will be \nin place through the middle of the week as Bermuda high pressure\npersists offshore. This will keep a very warm and moist airmass\nin place across the region through the period.\n\nHot and humid conditions will continue into the middle of the\nweek, although slightly lower temperatures and dewpoints will\narrive for Wednesday. Dewpoints are anticipated to mix out into\nthe mid to upper 60s again this afternoon. This will give some \nslight reprieve from the excessive humidity we've experienced in\nrecent days, though highs will still be in the upper 80s to mid\n90s. The lower dewpoints will result in maximum heat indices \nmainly in the mid to upper 90s, though could get near 100 \ndegrees in Delmarva and near the coast in New Jersey. Since the \nforecast is decidedly below criteria, we have opted to continue \nwithout a new Heat Advisory, though it will still be hot and \nhumid.\n\nThe faster synoptic pattern evolution described in the first \nsection above will continue to support slightly lower temperatures\nand heat index values beginning Tuesday. High temperatures in \nthe mid 80s to low 90s degrees for most Tuesday through Thursday,\nwith heat indices mainly in the mid 80s to mid 90s. This is closer\nto average for early to mid August.\n\nBy Friday and Saturday with the front finally having pushed through,\na much drier and more cooler airmass will arrive, which will bring\nmuch more pleasant weather conditions. Dewpoints may drop into the\n50s again for many areas with highs forecast to be in the low to\nmid 80s. Dewpoints look to start rebounding into the 60s again \nby Sunday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...There is potential for minor tidal flooding \nduring the evening high tides this week along the Atlantic \ncoast, Delaware Bay, and tidal Delaware River, especially on \nTuesday and Wednesday.\n\nAs we approach the New Moon on Wednesday, astronomical tides for\nthe evening high tide cycles will increase to around a half foot\nbelow minor flood stage in many areas this week along the \nAtlantic coasts of New Jersey and Delware, Delaware Bay, and \nthe tidal Delaware River. With tide departures running around a \nhalf foot above average, this will raise a threat of minor flood\nstage being reached for these areas.\n\nWe will continue to monitor the tide departures in the coming\ntotal water level forecasts. Coastal Flood Advisories may be\nneeded for a few zones if the departures become high enough, \nbut at least spotty minor tidal flooding should be anticipated \naround the times of the evening high tides this week. The \nhighest astronomical tides will occur on Wednesday, though \nTuesday's tides are of similar height. The threat of minor tidal\nflooding may linger through Thursday or Friday as well.\n\nTidal flooding is not currently forecast for the Eastern Shore\nof Chesapeake Bay.",
